ting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/4b/4b20823340d89e0ff4aa2fd65078d3819e8b218419a54ea632c1109e698858ab.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/cf/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256718 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fb80c1c-95cb-5565-a077-e01dae2538e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254362} {:answer/id #uuid "5575b773-fb45-568e-9e09-8e292ff7268e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6598c50e72417a0bff5514fa2b8ff9fba7c617cff10dc83e15472259d040846.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254415} {:answer/id #uuid "9d45adbb-a2ba-5573-a768-bab8773e9e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e30bba74fad98713f891950fd052d2ddd2cb2dc349bd9bcb7efe3333b7203dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255052} {:answer/id #uuid "bb73aff5-0f60-5f0e-a385-48945789fb36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255341} {:answer/id #uuid "f66620e0-e12e-5bd1-b12e-36f3f0afecb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f66620e0-e12e-5bd1-b12e-36f3f0afecb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255826} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d74b99d-539a-5a25-adea-708b48ca371b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256098}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef1fc6b0-b283-5ecf-84c4-88b300df87f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-122796" :question/problem "![](images/20/201804549f48637076fcaad5252632527b937e01cc75c153bc1db26b246d2aa6.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/f4/f4dc7eaf7ed7651ee38157c486f185ef3eecaf9ccb7e91df18e897c56c5c5e23.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/9c/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00678095-4785-5a48-bfd2-04906406b23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253671} {:answer/id #uuid "03aaca50-6ba1-5767-8a1d-61289921be5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4c/4c1a0035b37bc0137dc2ac164c980d538f9362feb3b439e663885f8aa7178124.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253699} {:answer/id #uuid "5936829d-c5a5-5317-93a8-8d046c07d07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fa8bf282264f1647644cc15b6aece745718bd17617afad2c2530292bfed10b15.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254452} {:answer/id #uuid "70f6299c-8313-5e6f-a9c7-80abdfba5fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/704a1e0712c0c2512e16ac03f1eeb7ecd55996c822eced54ac510f4719485c42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254671} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf9db65-02b1-5896-a31b-6345273456e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f9/f9d6304bc9cc6b6e74235c66a7d70b96dbea2db44e9ed82606f6025075b76336.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00678095-4785-5a48-bfd2-04906406b23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253671} :answer-field/id #uuid "a46a2d28-3dee-5856-b9b2-7ac4fb2b706b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256221}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f67d0b79-608f-51fc-afe3-9623997cda39" :question/math-academy-id "q-122810" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=-1,$ which gives the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=0,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=2,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/77/77a54ef31c41e0246e3d337a69c7faa161194058e6a5c672ef24d86866a83faf.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move downward and away from the origin $O.$ \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/4e/4e124d5e77ef9a28002aba9aac40f0b7eff1232dd1c08b6bca10b166469675c7.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n \n\n![](images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d16f010-8927-5a11-821b-dadc53e436d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3d5e0a4e1a1784167984a6dde093b97b14ebb0910ce56cd1d879782bfad6b7e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253779} {:answer/id #uuid "118abcd0-7890-5af2-b48d-3e8745d6c268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3fc34a2294e81abf04bfacf7d4367c0492a173af45f8f453a86b95a3a6028d4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253822} {:answer/id #uuid "6523a047-fedb-5a31-a148-d33413134901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b6b8dc1bcaf76374a580505308a9282b65e5da1b7d0961e2a6c30ba8ce6b2dd4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254577} {:answer/id #uuid "716fad33-4080-5fc3-bc31-939e1cf8b681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb66a357917b9683a61b04aeab03ff60e359160f3c5e51dc4b282d6464666467.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254676} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff449c6-cc37-5b79-8ef9-32639dce72bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5f/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ff449c6-cc37-5b79-8ef9-32639dce72bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5f/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254934} :answer-field/id #uuid "e493ba4b-c644-55d9-8213-f4543a4426f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256327}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d7e9bf88-89a8-589d-8711-7c4a07b86d32" :question/math-academy-id "q-122829" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the origin $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=1,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which gives an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=4,$ which gives an ellipse with semiaxes $4$ and $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/83/835b4206e04d064485d02635cfd4e2ad39a934b1d2b38296cc6463a265f5d931.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/cd/cd8e0a10d162d30be895fa4ce49e3944e4e1a3ee9f79aa2fafe70c0edd2c0d61.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/5f/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026f45f2-3480-501a-b813-16b2c86698bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/57f5858f1ccae96a169ee0aac3f106bd44b3de8282412389f4eb8d3ad8b66af9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253685} {:answer/id #uuid "27b268f0-4ffc-54a5-9920-488afedf2f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c5c0cc02a032916c8f9216673f23f3db81535828b14e51889942ffaf452ac3cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254023} {:answer/id #uuid "67bd03df-6493-53ce-b107-795c206a4430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e76b318c2f347bc59746ea0dc88c838ad46694724563867a0ee94318c826d887.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254608} {:answer/id #uuid "88069253-0215-50cc-b496-a51baed841a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/34/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254878} {:answer/id #uuid "9e64a607-79af-580d-9eca-019fa0b4a565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/08/08a6451e354eaaad23ef233a8c43d16512a2ed33257cd9e9b1257ef5eaaf558b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88069253-0215-50cc-b496-a51baed841a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/34/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254878} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e428f18-510c-5a07-b305-8e470386def5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255939}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43fea3a1-3b04-5702-a0ac-bdd3d7956101" :question/math-academy-id "q-122834" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=-1,$ which gives the vertical hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the pair of lines $y=\\pm x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2,$ which gives the horizontal hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the right half-plane $x>0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the left half-plane $x<0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/03/037f46d3fdd190749c2aefe51222475fdd337a7b3ce53e6809048e3491a31a47.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/34/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6926dfa-4ca0-5d9b-bb91-d663dc9fd39f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9b/9b8e151985327da40d1fb3044c7f4c785a9f0fcbaa5d71d7f865d4b3fee38ab9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383022} {:answer/id #uuid "2226c493-e827-540e-b916-5dff3335baf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/00/00b722a09fef4428d17fc7ff6a301b3860e798b20c44126b63cd381ad2884398.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383023} {:answer/id #uuid "1faef452-1968-5f42-b62d-394dc63eb8f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383024} {:answer/id #uuid "77afea4d-6f7d-5b97-a1b1-fcdcc7652a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/04f52ccb3eed1f0377edebf34607812f2c8eefe27e910e250959e8fa39ef13d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383025} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f42144-a862-5e50-bcf2-5c21c8fe56a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/ae9e6dc29361d66784dae2868e500940132049cfa04b65aafddc06d35c1afdbf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383026}] :answer-field/